  <p>Do your knees feel resistant on the first steps out of bed, or do your fingers take a while to move easily after a long drive? Joint stiffness after rest describes that difficulty getting a joint moving again. It can happen after sleep or a period of sitting. The feeling alone does not tell you why it is happening.</p>
  <p>The useful questions are whether it is new or recurring, how long it takes to ease, which joints are affected, and whether pain, swelling or warmth comes with it. Those details can help you decide when to get medical advice. For a broader introduction to maintaining mobility and understanding joint symptoms, see our <a href="/joint-health/">joint health guide</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
  <p>Stiffness after inactivity is described in several joint conditions. The <a href="#ref-niams">National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (NIAMS)</a> lists morning or after-rest stiffness among osteoarthritis symptoms; it usually lasts less than 30 minutes. The <a href="#ref-nhs-ra">NHS</a> says rheumatoid arthritis can also cause stiffness after inactivity, often with pain and swelling, and its morning stiffness often lasts longer. These are common patterns, not a way to identify your condition by watching a clock.</p>
  <p>Pay attention to the whole picture. A brief stiff feeling can still merit care if it is getting worse or interfering with daily life. Longer stiffness does not automatically mean inflammatory arthritis. A clinician can ask about your symptoms, examine the joints and decide whether further evaluation is needed.</p>

  <h2 id="patterns">Patterns to notice without diagnosing yourself</h2>
  <p>Note whether the stiffness follows a night's sleep, an hour at a desk, a car ride or another period of stillness. Is it in one knee or hip, or in several joints? Does it improve as you move, return after each rest, or remain troublesome through the day? Also note pain, visible swelling, warmth, difficulty making a fist, walking or climbing steps, and any unusual fatigue or fever. You do not need to decide which disease, if any, fits these observations.</p>
  <p>Morning stiffness is particularly useful to describe precisely. The NHS lists stiffness lasting more than 30 minutes after waking as a reason to arrange a non-urgent medical appointment. That is a prompt to seek assessment, not a test that separates osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis. The location of symptoms, swelling, course over time and examination matter too. The NHS notes that rheumatoid arthritis often affects small joints of the hands and feet and may affect both sides, but individual presentations vary.</p>

  <h2 id="what-to-do">What you can do while observing the pattern</h2>
  <p>If there are no urgent warning signs, try gentle movement within your comfort and avoid keeping the affected joint completely still. The <a href="#ref-nhs-pain">NHS joint-pain guidance</a> recommends regular exercise and says not to stop moving an affected joint entirely. Choose a manageable pace, and stop an activity if it sharply increases pain. If an injury or swelling is involved, the right approach may differ; ask a clinician or pharmacist about pain relief that is suitable for you.</p>
  <p>Keep a short record for a few days: the joint, when stiffness starts, roughly how long it lasts, what changes with movement, and whether it affects sleep or ordinary activities. A record helps you describe a recurring problem; it cannot confirm a diagnosis. Do not use a supplement as a substitute for evaluating persistent symptoms or a hot, swollen joint.</p>

  <h2 id="when-to-seek-care">When to seek care</h2>
  <p><strong>Arrange a routine appointment</strong> if pain or swelling worsens or keeps returning, affects sleep or normal activities, has not improved after two weeks of home care, or your joints stay stiff for more than 30 minutes after waking. Seek advice sooner if the change worries you, especially when several joints are affected or you notice swelling. These cues follow <a href="#ref-nhs-pain">NHS guidance</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Seek urgent medical help</strong> for joint pain with a hot, swollen area or if you feel generally unwell, feverish or shivery. A suddenly very painful, hot joint needs prompt assessment because infection is one possible cause. <strong>Seek emergency care after an injury</strong> if pain is severe, you cannot walk or put weight on the joint, it looks out of place, or you have numbness or tingling nearby. Use your local urgent or emergency service; the source's UK phone numbers may not apply where you live.</p>

  <h2 id="questions">Common questions</h2>
  <h3>Is morning stiffness always arthritis?</h3>
  <p>No. Morning stiffness is a symptom, not a diagnosis. Osteoarthritis and inflammatory arthritis are among possible explanations, and other causes of joint pain or stiffness exist. A health professional can consider the pattern and examine you.</p>
  <h3>Does the 30-minute mark tell me which kind of arthritis I have?</h3>
  <p>No. It is a useful detail to report and, per the NHS, a reason to seek routine assessment when morning stiffness lasts longer than that. Duration by itself cannot confirm or rule out a particular condition.</p>

